; T15 - pen-cycle grid.  10 x 14 = 140 ticks, each one a full pen down / draw /
; pen up cycle.  About 45 x 68 mm and roughly 6 minutes.
;
; THIS IS THE BREAK-FINDING TEST.  The suspected fault is the servo actuating
; after a move, so this does nothing else: 140 pen cycles, back to back, with
; the shortest possible move between them.  If the machine resets, it resets
; here.
;
; HOW TO REPORT WHERE IT BROKE - just count the marks on the paper.
;   Ticks are drawn in rows of 10, bottom row first, and the rows alternate
;   direction: row 1 left to right, row 2 right to left, and so on.
;   Count complete rows, then ticks into the part-finished row:
;       tick number = (complete rows x 10) + ticks into the current row
;   So 'four full rows and then three more' is tick 43, and that is the
;   number to tell Claude.  A half-drawn or smeared tick counts - say so.
;
; The paper is the record, not the console.  plot2.py's counter shows lines
; ACCEPTED into the buffer, which runs several seconds ahead of the pen, and a
; reset loses whatever was still in flight.
;
; Relative (G91), so it needs no work offset and never goes near X0 Y0.  It
; grows RIGHT and AWAY from where the pen starts, and returns to the start
; point at the end.  Put the pen at the near-left corner of your paper.
;
; Pen: S140 up, S90 down.  Soft-started from pen down.
